  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## Adjectives in English – grammar chart\n\n![Colorful educational chart explaining the use of adjectives in English grammar with examples in sentences, showing adjective-noun combinations, the use of adjectives with 'am/is/are', sensory verbs, and the rule against pluralizing adjectives.](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-1.png)\n\n#### Adjective + noun\n\nWhen we use adjectives with a noun in English, the adjectives go **before the noun**.\n\n-   *It’s an **expensive guitar**. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-2.png)* \n-   *It’s a **guitar** **expensive**. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-3.png)* \n-   *She has a **new car**. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-4.png)* \n-   *She has a **car** **new**. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-5.png)* \n\n#### **Am/is/are** + adjective\n\nWe can also use adjectives without a noun after the verb **[be](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/present-simple-forms-of-to-be/)**.\n\n-   *This guitar **is expensive**.* \n-   *Her car **is new**.* \n\nIn questions, the adjective can go after **be** + subject.\n\n-   *Is your car **new**?* \n-   *Are your classmates **nice**?* \n\n#### **Feel/look/smell/sound/taste** + adjective\n\nWe can also use adjectives without a noun after the [verbs of the senses](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/b1-b2/verbs-of-the-senses/): **feel, look, smell, sound,** **taste**.\n\n-   *Your idea **sounds interesting**.* \n-   *I don’t like this soup. It **tastes horrible**.*\n-   *You **look tired**.* \n-   *These cookies **smell delicious**.* \n-   *I **feel depressed**.* \n\n#### No plural\n\nAdjectives in English have **no plural**. They are always singular.\n\n-   *These are my **favourite** shoes. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-6.png)* \n-   *These are my **favourites** shoes.* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-7.png)*\n-   *She has **blue** eyes. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-8.png)* \n-   *She has **blues** eyes. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/adjectives/img-9.png)* \n\n#### **Very/quite**\n\nWe can use **very** and **quite** before the adjectives.\n\n-   *It’s **very** expensive.*\n-   *It’s **quite** expensive*.",
